e a tremendous difference just  keep it up because this is going to get  us out of it this is a best and only  great public health tool but the former  head of the FDA now in formal White  House coronavirus advisor Scott Gottlieb  spilled the beans on Sunday while  mitigation didn't fail I think it's fair  to say that it didn't work as well as we  expected we expected that we would start  seeing more significant declines in new  cases and deaths around the nation at  this point and we're just not seeing  that well although intuitively I think  it probably seemed like social  distancing would be necessary there was  no real scientific basis for belief  that since it never been studied and  it's one infectious disease doctor told  me last week trying to stop this virus  with social distancing is like trying to  drive a nail through jello viruses  spread that's what they do they often  weaken as they go and if it's like SARS  we hope it is it'll eventually burn out  as SARS did an observational study by a  researcher at Woods Hole Oceanographic  Institution not yet peer-reviewed  examined whether stay at home orders  actually prevented deaths in Western  Europe comparing the trajectory of the  epidemic before and after the lockdown  we find no evidence of any discontinuity  in the growth rate doubling time and  reproduction number trends we provide  estimates of the death toll in the  absence of any lockdown policies and  show that these strategies might not  have saved any life in Western Europe  well if true I say if that would be  stunning and the author does echo the  sentiments of Nobel laureate Michael  Leavitt of Stanford University what's  your view of the lockdown policy that so  many European countries and states in  America have introduced I think it is a  huge mistake I think that everybody  panicked they were fed incorrect numbers  by epidemiologists there's no doubt in  my mind when we come to look back on  this the damage done by lockdown will  exceed any saving or glides by a huge  factor that's what I heard today at my  own doctor's office because no one's  there and as the public wants answers  while some of the experts are hailing  the results of new expensive drug  therapies with middling evidence of  success but warning us away from cheap  generic drugs that have already saved  lives both here and abroad and the FDA I  hate to say it but they got this one  wrong big time well of course every drug  has potential side effects and in  prescription drug interactions there's  more than ample evidence at  hydroxychloroquine saves lives speeds up  recovery time and cuts down on  hospitalizations  it's also one of the safer drugs out  there just by virtue of the fact that  it's been around for what 65 years and  as I've mentioned before Frances  esteemed epidemiologist professor Didier  rahu and his team of colleagues release  the results of hydroxychloroquine in a  thousand 61 kovat patients good clinical  outcome and very logical cure were  obtained in 973 patients within ten days  no cardiac toxicity it showed 91%  effectiveness in eight deaths so why is  the FDA not so much as noted the work of  this premier epidemiologist in all of  Europe and by the way Chinese  researchers release their own  retrospective study on Friday and they  found equally impressive results using  hydroxychloroquine in patients who were  seriously ill 568 patients critically  ill with kovat all received basic  therapy but additionally 48 of them  received hydroxychloroquine for 7 to 10  days 200 milligrams twice per day  hydroxychloroquine treatment is  significantly associated with decreased  mortality in critically ill patients  with Kovan 19 through attenuation of  inflammatory cytokine storm  therefore hydroxychloroquine should be  prescribed for treatment of critically  ill Koba 19 patients to save lives  ok well we can say consider the source  this is from China and it's not yet  peer-reviewed but in a pandemic as  Professor Rowe has said with lives on  the line and still no established  therapy or vaccine not everything can be  peer-reviewed experts are not always  right none of us are it can't be no  one's infallible but in times like these  sometimes we have to take yes for an  answer  now finally about all of those bad  models the experts were all over the  place and projecting the virus's  lethality we're going to bring down the  number of infections so we don't have to  point to million deaths or 1.6 million  deaths I wouldn't be surprised if we saw  a hundred thousand deaths it's more like  the 60,000 then 100 to 200,000  okay if you're not confused yet now  they're saying that a hundred and twenty  hundred thirty four thousand could die  bike Ovid by August of what that's  double what they were saying a few weeks  ago so why is this happening well the  expert at IH NV a University of  Washington did a lot of this modeling he  explained it this way we're seeing many  states reporting on presumptive deaths  that were missed in the official death  data and now they're going back finding  deaths that were in nursing homes elder  care facilities and reporting you know  large numbers of those deaths on a given  day that just means that more deaths  occurred that means the trajectory of  the epidemic is also bigger ah  retrospectively adding death to the  covin count no issues there I'm sure no  issues with accuracy except that we had  one of the worst influenza pneumonia  seasons in decades in the United States  not reported very often is it so we're  still trying to get a final count on  those flu and pneumonia deaths from the  CDC by the way haven't gotten them yet  very very strange well the experts  strike again  and those are my thoughts at the end of  day 49 America in shut down

